  "generated_text": "To find the modulo \\(6\\) remainder of the sum \\(1 + 2 + 3 + \\cdots + 99 + 100\\), we start by determining the sum of the first 100 positive integers. The formula for the sum of the first \\(n\\) positive integers is:\n\n\\[\nS = \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n = 100\\), the sum \\(S\\) is:\n\n\\[\nS = \\frac{100 \\times 101}{2} = 5050\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to find the remainder when \\(5050\\) is divided by \\(6\\). We perform the division \\(5050 \\div 6\\):\n\n\\[\n5050 \\div 6 = 841 \\text{ remainder } 4\n\\]\n\nThis can be verified by the calculation:\n\n\\[\n5050 = 6 \\times 841 + 4\n\\]\n\nThus, the remainder when \\(5050\\) is divided by \\(6\\) is indeed \\(4\\). Therefore, the modulo \\(6\\) remainder of the sum \\(1 + 2 + 3 + \\cdots + 99 + 100\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4}\n\\]",
